    asks = "The other way to make small text survive arbitrary icon art. An outline fights the background at every glyph edge; a plate REPLACES the background, so the label is read against one known colour and the stroke stops having to do the work. It is also the badge's own move — `tokens.badges.plate` is why a 22 px sprite reads on any icon — so the row already contains this idea and the hint would be echoing it rather than introducing it. What it costs is ink: a filled rectangle in the corner is a second object on the row, and the whole claim of chrome is that it recedes. Does it still recede?",
    cells = {
      {
        ability = "Eye Beam",
        caption = "the <b>widest</b> label, on dark art. If a candidate fails anywhere it fails here.",
        key = "csF1",
        verdict = "press",
